{"causes": ["The ongoing Anglo-Norman expansion into Ireland provided opportunities for ambitious nobles like Geoffrey to secure land and influence through strategic marriages and military service.", "Geoffrey's marriage to Maud de Lacy around 1252 granted him the Lordship of Trim, establishing his substantial power base and influence in the Lordship of Ireland.", "The political instability of the Second Barons' War in England compelled nobles to choose loyalties, leading Geoffrey to align with the royalist cause under Henry III and Edward I."], "effects": ["Geoffrey's tenure as Justiciar of Ireland from 1273 to 1276 significantly strengthened English royal administration and law enforcement in the Lordship.", "His military campaigns and landholdings in Meath contributed to the further entrenchment of Anglo-Norman power and the suppression of Gaelic Irish autonomy.", "The legacy of his baronial authority and strategic importance endured through his descendants, particularly the Mortimer family, who inherited the Lordship of Trim."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "geoffrey-of-joinville", "sourceName": "Geoffrey of Joinville", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "seventh-crusade", "targetName": "Seventh Crusade", "context": "Geoffrey of Joinville participated in the Seventh Crusade (1248–1254) alongside Louis IX of France."}, {"sourceSlug": "henry-iii-of-england", "sourceName": "Henry III of England", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "geoffrey-of-joinville", "targetName": "Geoffrey of Joinville", "context": "Henry III appointed Geoffrey of Joinville as Lord of Trim and relied on his loyalty during the Second Barons' War, shaping his early career in England and Ireland."}, {"sourceSlug": "geoffrey-of-joinville", "sourceName": "Geoffrey of Joinville", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "trim-county-meath-ireland", "targetName": "Trim, County Meath, Ireland", "context": "Geoffrey of Joinville held the Lordship of Trim and resided primarily at Trim Castle, making it the center of his power and administration."}, {"sourceSlug": "geoffrey-of-joinville", "sourceName": "Geoffrey of Joinville", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "edward-i-of-england", "targetName": "Edward I of England", "context": "Geoffrey of Joinville served as a loyal supporter and official, including Justiciar of Ireland, for Edward I from 1273 until his retirement."}, {"sourceSlug": "geoffrey-of-joinville", "sourceName": "Geoffrey of Joinville", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "lordship-of-trim", "targetName": "Lordship of Trim", "context": "Geoffrey of Joinville, through his daughter Joan, transmitted the Lordship of Trim to his grandson Roger Mortimer, 2nd Baron Mortimer of Wigmore."}], "places": [{"name": "Trim, County Meath, Ireland", "role": "Primary Lordship & Residence"}, {"name": "Paris, France", "role": "Early life & Crusader service"}, {"name": "London, England", "role": "Political center & Parliamentary attendance"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Geoffrey of Joinville", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "geoffrey-of-joinville"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1503067", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geoffrey_de_Geneville,_1st_Baron_Geneville"], "tags": ["Political Leaders", "political-systems", "geopolitical-linkage", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Geoffrey of Joinville's role as Justiciar of Ireland and Baron of Trim significantly consolidated Anglo-Norman power in Meath and strengthened English administrative structures in the 13th-century Lordship of Ireland, impacting regional governance for centuries. His career exemplifies the Anglo-French aristocratic network that shaped medieval British Isles.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-24T16:15:29Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45434086161", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Geoffrey de Geneville, 1st Baron Geneville also known as Geoffrey de Joinville and
Geoffroi de Joinville, was an Anglo-French noble, supporter of Henry III, who appointed him Baron of Trim, County Meath, and, subsequently, a staunch supporter of Edward I.", "newValue": "Geoffrey de Joinville (c. 1226 – October 21, 1314), also known as Geoffroi de Geneville, was a prominent Anglo-French noble and Crusader whose career spanned the reigns of three English monarchs and deeply intertwined with the political landscape of 13th and early 14th-century England and Ireland. Hailing from the powerful House of Joinville in Champagne, France, his strategic marriage into the Lacy family established him as Lord of Trim in County Meath, Ireland, where he became a pivotal figure in the Anglo-Norman administration and expansion. His military prowess and diplomatic service rendered him a trusted confidant and loyal supporter of both Henry III and his son, Edward I, particularly during the Second Barons' War and the Edwardian conquests.

Born into a cadet branch of the illustrious Joinville family, Geoffrey was nephew to the renowned chronicler Jean de Joinville. His fortunes shifted significantly upon his marriage to Maud de Lacy, daughter and co-heir of Gilbert de Lacy, around 1252. This union brought him the Lordship of Trim and its substantial associated lands in the Lordship of Ireland, along with the title 1st Baron Geneville. He participated in the Seventh Crusade (1248–1254) alongside Louis IX of France and later served Henry III against Simon de Montfort during the Second Barons' War (1264–1267), enduring imprisonment after the Battle of Lewes. Under Edward I, he held significant offices, including Justiciar of Ireland from 1273 to 1276, where he actively pursued the expansion of English royal authority and the suppression of local Gaelic resistance. His later years involved further military service in Wales and Scotland, and he was summoned to Parliament as Baron Geneville from 1295 until 1307."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-24T16:15:29Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45434086161", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1226, http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/6891aa57948683517676aaca8e6b2b5b"}, {"title": "Political crises and power transitions requiring new leadership", "type": "Idea", "year": "1226, http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/6891aa57948683517676aaca8e6b2b5b"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Anglo-Norman expansion into Ireland provided opportunities for ambitious nobles like Geoffrey to secure land and influence through strategic marriages and military service.", "Geoffrey's marriage to Maud de Lacy around 1252 granted him the Lordship of Trim, establishing his substantial power base and influence in the Lordship of Ireland.", "The political instability of the Second Barons' War in England compelled nobles to choose loyalt